Subject: Cantor Resumable Coordination Checkpoint P0 Justification

@ [justification_uuid] bded1938-2845-472b-9b82-c811fa3d710e
@ [canonical] C:\Project\Cantor\specifications\Cantor_Resumable_Coordination_Checkpoint_P0.sop

& [Need] is whole-program rather than per-process reentry
  + existing ProcessInstanceState and SerializedContinuation already cover variables instruction cursor and yielded process state
  + current coordination execution still retains scheduler queues traces messages returns wake sets and other process states only inside one host call
  + serializing only one process would lose information required to select the next deterministic transition

& [Leverage] reuses the mature interpreter
  + exposing the full scheduler frontier adds chunking without inventing a second attention bytecode
  + identical terminal outcomes demonstrate that checkpoint partitioning is transport scheduling rather than semantic change
  + later tools can operate on this pure seam without modifying llama.cpp

& [Safety] is exact and finite
  + every resume revalidates original authority and input lineage
  + caller quota is positive finite and subordinate to existing invocation and IR bounds
  + content hashes catch accidental drift while explicit nonclaims prevent them from becoming authentication

& [AlternativesRejected] keeps architecture coherent
  - native stack snapshots are nonportable unsafe and already prohibited
  - a new interpreter would split semantics and proof
  - persistence before a pure restart value would combine crash consistency with language semantics
  - subroutines before resumable scheduler state would add control complexity without fixing the current all-at-once host boundary

& [Conclusion] is implement the pure checkpoint stepper before call-stack MCP persistence or model-host integration
